{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Qo4adosUxbn6s3c33rdpfx","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I have all the requested files. Here's the complete report:\n\n## Code Style Rules - Full Contents\n\n### 1. Go Style Constitution\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5659/docs/constitutions/go-style.yaml`\n\nThis comprehensive guide defines Go coding standards and design patterns for the project, covering:\n\n**Key Sections:**\n- **Copyright Header:** Every file must start with SPDX copyright header (lines 1-2)\n- **Duplication:** Extract common logic at the two-occurrence threshold; check for existing code before writing new functions\n- **Implementation Strategy:** Write all source files before running build/test commands; plan full implementation mentally first to minimize turns\n- **Design Patterns:** Eight patterns documented - Strategy, Command, Facade, Factory, Decorator, Builder, Adapter, Observer\n- **Interfaces:** Every boundary between parent and internal sub-packages must be defined by an interface, even with single implementations. Keep interfaces small (1-3 methods)\n- **Struct & Function Design:** One concept per struct, one thing per function. Group >3 parameters into config struct. Split functions >200 lines\n- **Receiver Conventions:** Use pointer receivers for stateful/large types, value receivers for small immutables. Use 1-2 letter abbreviations (never \"self\" or \"this\")\n- **Error Handling:** Handle at point of occurrence with guard clauses. Wrap errors with context using fmt.Errorf. Never silently discard without comment\n- **Panic vs Error:** Return errors for runtime conditions. Use panic only for programming contract violations\n- **Init Functions:** Avoid init(). Use only for side-effect registration or infallible lookups\n- **No Magic Strings:** Centralize all string literals as named constants (binary names, paths, URLs)\n- **Configuration via YAML:** All config through configuration.yaml. Never use os.Getenv() for orchestrator behavior\n- **Constants:** Use typed constants for fixed sets. Use iota for sequential enums\n- **Project Structure:** cmd/ (entry points), internal/ (private), pkg/ (public API), tests/ (integration), magefiles/ (build)\n- **File Organization:** Name by concern (scaffold.go, stitch.go), not by kind (no types.go, helpers.go)\n- **Import Organization:** Three groups (stdlib, external, internal) separated by blank lines\n- **Standard Packages:** mage, cobra, viper, testify, yaml.v3, encoding/json, google/uuid\n- **Struct Embedding:** Extract shared fields into common struct\n- **Naming Conventions:** PascalCase (exported), camelCase (unexported), kebab-case (CLI flags), binXxx (binaries), NewXxx (factories)\n- **Comment Style:** Godoc comments on all exported symbols starting with symbol name\n- **Concurrency:** Thread context.Context through I/O paths. Protect mutable state with sync.Mutex\n- **Testing:** Table-driven tests with t.Run(). Call t.Parallel() on safe tests. Call t.Helper() on helpers\n- **Code Review Checklist:** 20-point checklist covering duplication, magic strings, error handling, single responsibility, function size, interfaces, boolean parameters, imports, godoc, init() usage, panic safety, iota enums, and incremental compilation\n\n### 2. Testing Constitution\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5659/docs/constitutions/testing.yaml`\n\nFive core testing principles:\n\n- **T1 - Hierarchical Test Naming:** `Test{Suite}_UC{NNN}_DescriptiveName` format enabling filtering at suite, use case, or test level\n- **T2 - One File Per Use Case:** Tests in `uc{NNN}_{slug}_test.go` files. Shared infrastructure in helpers_test.go\n- **T3 - Spec-to-Code Traceability:** Every test spec entry must reference a test function and vice versa (no orphans)\n- **T4 - Test Count Invariant:** Function count must be >= spec entry count (may exceed for edge cases/performance tests, never fall below)\n- **T5 - Mage Target Naming:** Expose as `(Test) Uc{NNN}{UseCaseSlug}()` plus test:usecase, test:unit, test:all targets\n\n### 3. cmd/pr/ Directory Contents\n\n**main.go** (267 lines): Implements a `pr` utility (srd110-pr R1.1, R2.1, R2.2, R2.3) that paginates text input with headers and footers. Features:\n- Flag parsing for `--length`, `--header`, `--omit-header` (-t), `--omit-pagination` (-T)\n- SIGPIPE handler installation\n- Page formatting with configurable header text, page numbers, dates\n- Support for stdin and file input\n- Default page length of 66 lines, default width of 72 characters\n\n**pr_test.go** (137 lines): Differential test suite comparing output against reference binary (`gpr`). Tests:\n- stdin handling (with/without dash)\n- header omission (short/long flags)\n- pagination control\n- custom page lengths\n- custom headers\n- combined flag scenarios\n- 18 total test cases (r2_3_stdin_no_file through r2_3_file_arg)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"b8ba7146-ce80-464f-81bc-b722a4bdfcb1","uuid":"aaefb834-1f5f-4d60-8385-257f8183921f","timestamp":"2026-05-29T19:41:32.047Z","tool_use_result":{"status":"completed","prompt":"I need to understand the code style rules for this Go codebase. Read these files and report their full contents:\n\n1. /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5659/docs/constitutions/go_style_constitution.yaml - Go style rules\n2. /private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5659/docs/constitutions/go_testing_constitution.yaml - Testing rules if it exists\n\nAlso check for any existing cmd/pr/ directory contents.\n\nReport the FULL contents of each file.","agentId":"ab058fb42c6073f90","agentType":"Explore","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I have all the requested files. Here's the complete report:\n\n## Code Style Rules - Full Contents\n\n### 1. Go Style Constitution\n**File:** `/private/var/folders/dl/cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn/T/go-unix-utils-worktrees/5659/docs/constitutions/go-style.yaml`\n\nThis comprehensive guide defines Go coding standards and design patterns for the project, covering:\n\n**Key Sections:**\n- **Copyright Header:** Every file must start with SPDX copyright header (lines 1-2)\n- **Duplication:** Extract common logic at the two-occurrence threshold; check for existing code before writing new functions\n- **Implementation Strategy:** Write all source files before running build/test commands; plan full implementation mentally first to minimize turns\n- **Design Patterns:** Eight patterns documented - Strategy, Command, Facade, Factory, Decorator, Builder, Adapter, Observer\n- **Interfaces:** Every boundary between parent and internal sub-packages must be defined by an interface, even with single implementations. Keep interfaces small (1-3 methods)\n- **Struct & Function Design:** One concept per struct, one thing per function. Group >3 parameters into config struct. Split functions >200 lines\n- **Receiver Conventions:** Use pointer receivers for stateful/large types, value receivers for small immutables. Use 1-2 letter abbreviations (never \"self\" or \"this\")\n- **Error Handling:** Handle at point of occurrence with guard clauses. Wrap errors with context using fmt.Errorf. Never silently discard without comment\n- **Panic vs Error:** Return errors for runtime conditions. Use panic only for programming contract violations\n- **Init Functions:** Avoid init(). Use only for side-effect registration or infallible lookups\n- **No Magic Strings:** Centralize all string literals as named constants (binary names, paths, URLs)\n- **Configuration via YAML:** All config through configuration.yaml. Never use os.Getenv() for orchestrator behavior\n- **Constants:** Use typed constants for fixed sets. Use iota for sequential enums\n- **Project Structure:** cmd/ (entry points), internal/ (private